# APP — the operations that BUILD an application, and nothing else.
#
# `repair.mf` fixes code that already exists. This file writes code that does not, and it is the same
# machinery pointed the other way: a goal, a driver, and operations whose PARAMETER TYPE is their
# precondition.
#
# ⭐ THE CLAIM THIS FILE EXISTS TO MAKE. The old engine derived a feature set by forward chaining — assert
# facts, saturate, read off what became true — and a separate composer then turned features into code. The
# new engine has no forward chaining at all. What replaces it is here: a business decision is an operation
# whose argument must already satisfy a type, so `grant_discount(b: qualified_build)` is PROPOSABLE only
# against a build that has been qualified, and the planner rediscovers the dependency order by itself.
#
#     THE PLAN IS THE DERIVATION.
#
# That is strictly better than the saturated graph it replaces, and not because it is faster: a saturated
# graph says what became true, while a plan says WHAT WAS DONE, IN ORDER, AND WHY EACH STEP WAS AVAILABLE.
# The audit trail is the artifact rather than a reconstruction of one.
#
# ⚠ EVERY OPERATION HERE IS GRAPH SURGERY ON A REAL AST, never string assembly. The skeleton and every
# fragment were PARSED by `pystrider.intake`, so each carries `from_code`, an `origin` and a `source_line`,
# and what these operations splice together is an artifact rather than our intention about one. Emission
# then goes through `ast.unparse`, so the result is valid Python by construction — indentation, precedence
# and statement order are not ours to get wrong.
#
# ⚠ WHAT IS AUTHORED AND WHAT IS DERIVED, stated plainly so nobody reads more into this than it earns.
# The BODIES of the fragments are authored Python — this is not a system that invents `_show_discount`.
# What is derived is WHICH fragments exist in the emitted app and how they are assembled, and that is
# derived from the knobs by a search that could have gone otherwise. The seams are named
# (`_present`, `_finish`), every seam is filled by exactly one fragment, and which one is the plan's
# answer, not a template's.

# --- the business decision ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Does this cart earn the discount? Reads the knobs and writes the verdict — the ONLY arithmetic in the
# derivation, because the ISA has `LT` and `EQ` and that is genuinely all this question needs.
#
# ⚠ It writes `qualifies` on BOTH branches, and the false branch is load-bearing. `install_plain_display`
# takes an `unqualified_build`, which is `qualifies = false` — an ATTRIBUTE THAT IS PRESENT AND FALSE, not
# an absent one. If this returned early instead, a build that had never been qualified would be
# indistinguishable from one that had been found wanting, and the planner could install the plain display
# before asking the business question at all.
fn qualify(b: build) -> build:
    INVOKE R(tier) slot_of node=F(b) key="tier"
    INVOKE R(spend) slot_of node=F(b) key="spend"
    INVOKE R(threshold) slot_of node=F(b) key="threshold"

    EQ R(loyal) R(tier) "premium"
    JMPNOT R(loyal) .no

    LT R(under) R(spend) R(threshold)
    JMPIF R(under) .no

    INVOKE R(_) set_slot node=F(b) key="qualifies" value=true
    JMP .done

.no:
    INVOKE R(_) set_slot node=F(b) key="qualifies" value=false

.done:
    CONST R(end) true

# ⭐ The decision REACHES INTO THE CODE. Granting the discount is not a flag the emitter later consults —
# it rewrites the `APPLIES = False` constant in the skeleton's own AST, exactly the way `lower_threshold`
# rewrites a comparison's constant in `repair.mf`. So there is no separate "apply the decisions" pass that
# could disagree with the decisions: applying IS deciding.
fn grant_discount(b: qualified_build) -> discounting_build:
    INVOKE R(c) related node=F(b) label="applies_constant"
    INVOKE R(_) set_slot node=R(c) key="value" value=true
    INVOKE R(_) set_slot node=F(b) key="discount" value=true

# --- the `_present` seam --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two operations, mutually exclusive BY TYPE rather than by an `if`. Neither is proposable until `qualify`
# has run, and afterwards exactly one is.

fn install_discount_display(b: discounting_build) -> displaying_build:
    INVOKE R(f) related node=F(b) label="discount_display"
    INVOKE R(blk) related node=F(b) label="class_block"
    INVOKE R(_) relate node=R(blk) label="stmt" other=R(f)
    INVOKE R(_) set_slot node=F(b) key="displays" value=true
    INVOKE R(_) set_slot node=F(b) key="shows_discount" value=true

fn install_plain_display(b: unqualified_build) -> displaying_build:
    INVOKE R(f) related node=F(b) label="plain_display"
    INVOKE R(blk) related node=F(b) label="class_block"
    INVOKE R(_) relate node=R(blk) label="stmt" other=R(f)
    INVOKE R(_) set_slot node=F(b) key="displays" value=true
    INVOKE R(_) set_slot node=F(b) key="shows_discount" value=false

# --- the `_finish` seam ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ THE SAFETY PROPERTY LIVES IN THESE TWO PARAMETER TYPES, and it took two corrections to state honestly.
#
# An irreversible checkout can only be finished by `install_gated_finish`, because the direct one demands
# `irreversible = false`. The first version of this comment said the unsafe app was therefore UNBUILDABLE.
# It is not. A parameter type is enforced by `driver.proposals`, which is a PLANNING constraint: it is why
# no plan reaches the unsafe app, and it does nothing whatever about a caller who invokes the operation
# directly, because `function.invoke` does not check parameter types at all. Caught by a pin that asserted
# the invocation would raise and watched it succeed.
#
# So the guarantee is stated in two parts, and the `NATIVE "check"` below is the second one:
#
#   * NO PLAN builds the unsafe app — carried by the parameter type, at proposal time.
#   * NO CALL builds it either — carried by the check, at invocation time, which is the only thing that
#     makes the declared type binding on someone who bypasses the planner.
#
# ⚠ This was the `CHECK` opcode until ugm's 2026-08-02 restructuring retired `PLAN`/`STEP`/`CHECK`: the
# instruction set imported `driver` and `types` to serve them, so the kernel knew what a plan and a type
# were, and a port would have had to port both to implement three instructions. They are now primitives
# reached BY NAME through `native.py`, which the kernel does not populate. Same semantics — it still
# answers by raising, not by returning, which is why there is no destination register here.
#
# And both together are still only as good as what the types SAY. Which is why driving the emitted app
# through Pilot is not a formality: it is the independent observation that the derivation was right about
# the real toolkit. `experiments/strider_app.py` deliberately relaxes this type to prove the drive would
# have caught what the derivation stopped preventing.

fn install_direct_finish(b: reversible_build) -> finishing_build:
    NATIVE "check" F(b) "reversible_build"
    INVOKE R(f) related node=F(b) label="direct_finish"
    INVOKE R(blk) related node=F(b) label="class_block"
    INVOKE R(_) relate node=R(blk) label="stmt" other=R(f)
    INVOKE R(_) set_slot node=F(b) key="finishes" value=true
    INVOKE R(_) set_slot node=F(b) key="gated" value=false

# The gate brings its own screen, and the screen goes on the MODULE rather than in the class — the one
# operation here that writes to two places, because a confirmation gate is genuinely two pieces of code.
fn install_gated_finish(b: irreversible_build) -> finishing_build:
    NATIVE "check" F(b) "irreversible_build"
    INVOKE R(f) related node=F(b) label="gated_finish"
    INVOKE R(blk) related node=F(b) label="class_block"
    INVOKE R(_) relate node=R(blk) label="stmt" other=R(f)
    INVOKE R(scr) related node=F(b) label="confirm_screen"
    INVOKE R(mod) related node=F(b) label="module_node"
    INVOKE R(_) relate node=R(mod) label="defines" other=R(scr)
    INVOKE R(_) set_slot node=F(b) key="finishes" value=true
    INVOKE R(_) set_slot node=F(b) key="gated" value=true
